+{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"text":"Let me think through the algorithms carefully.\n\n**Part 1 (Toggle Puzzle):** This is a system of linear equations over GF(2). Each light gives one equation: the XOR of buttons affecting that light must equal the target. We need the solution with minimum Hamming weight (fewest 1s), and lexicographically smallest among ties.\n\nWith up to 40 buttons, we can use Gaussian elimination to find the null space, then enumerate over the null space to find the minimum-weight solution. The null space dimension is at most 40, but in practice much smaller. If the null space has dimension k, we enumerate 2^k combinations — this is feasible when k is small.\n\n**Part 2 (Counter Puzzle):** This is a system of linear equations over Z_p (prime field). Same approach: Gaussian elimination, find null space, enumerate over null space to find minimum total presses.\n\nLet me implement both:\n\n","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_q27_617","model":"qwopus-27b-mtp","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":24724,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dcbb6a1-434c-4a36-bc37-9a37e88a909b","uuid":"a98978af-80c3-4a86-97f3-943fb33ba9f6"}
+{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_q27_617_0","input":{"content":"// Factory Reset: Button-toggle and Counter puzzles\n\n// ─── Part 1: Toggle Puzzle ───────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\ninterface Machine {\n  target: boolean[];\n  buttons: number[][];\n}\n\n/**\n * Solve the toggle puzzle.\n *\n * This is a system of linear equations over GF(2).\n * - N lights (variables: which lights to toggle)\n * - M buttons (each button toggles a subset of lights)\n * - Each button pressed 0 or 1 time\n * - Find minimum number of button presses to reach target\n *\n * Approach:\n * 1. Build augmented matrix [A | b] over GF(2)\n * 2. Gaussian elimination to find a particular solution + null space\n * 3. Enumerate null space combinations (2^k where k = null space dimension)\n * 4. Pick the solution with minimum Hamming weight, then lexicographically smallest\n */\nfunction solveToggle(machine: Machine): number[] | null {\n  const n = machine.target.length; // number of lights\n  const m = machine.buttons.length; // number of buttons\n\n  // Build matrix: n rows (lights), m columns (buttons)\n  // A[i][j] = 1 if button j toggles light i\n  const A: number[][] = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    const row = new Array(m).fill(0);\n    for (const j of machine.buttons) {\n      if (j === i) {\n        // This doesn't work — buttons[j] is the list of lights button j toggles\n      }\n    }\n    A.push(row);\n  }\n  // Correct: A[i][j] = 1 if button j toggles light i\n  for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n    for (const light of machine.buttons[j]) {\n      A[light][j] = 1;\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Target vector b\n  const b: number[] = machine.target.map(v => (v ? 1 : 0));\n\n  // Augmented matrix: n rows, m+1 columns (last column is b)\n  const aug: number[][] = A.map((row, i) => [...row, b[i]]);\n\n  // Gaussian elimination over GF(2)\n  const pivotCol = new Array(n).fill(-1); // pivotCol[row] = column of pivot\n  const pivotRow = new Array(m).fil